  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25b-perchlike-fishes-2-cichlids-from-the-great-african-lakes/neolamprologus_meeli_en/",
      "html": "<p>Male become around 7 cm in length, females stay about 2 cm smaller. Available size: 5-7 cm. Origin: captive bred.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25b-perchlike-fishes-2-cichlids-from-the-great-african-lakes/neolamprologus_meeli_en/",
      "html": "<p>Neolamprologus meeli was described in 1948 by Max Poll from a place near Katibili, which belongs to the Congolese part of Lake Tanganyika.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25b-perchlike-fishes-2-cichlids-from-the-great-african-lakes/neolamprologus_meeli_en/",
      "html": "<p>In this species mainly the females use the empty snail shell as a hiding place. It is a perfect tankmate for any Tanganyika aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25b-perchlike-fishes-2-cichlids-from-the-great-african-lakes/neolamprologus_meeli_en/",
      "html": "<p>Males become around 7 cm in length, females stay about 2 cm smaller.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25b-perchlike-fishes-2-cichlids-from-the-great-african-lakes/neolamprologus_meeli_en/",
      "html": "<p>Among the snail-dwelling cichlids of Lake Tanganyika are some species that look very much alike, and in some cases it remains unclear whether the scientific name applied to them is correct. The species is very similar to N. boulengeri and N. hecqui; the three species have in common the black borders of the fins.</p><p>There is a lot of discussion whether the fish kept and bred in the hobby under the name Neolamprologus or Lepidolamprologus meeli \"Zambia\" really belongs to this species or represents a colour variety of hecqui.</p>"
    }
  ],
